In C++ array passed to function as pointer, so no bound checks are performed. For example:

```c++
int a[3] = {1, 2, 3};

void accept(int v[4]) {
   v[3] = 1;
}

accept(a);   // Compiles ok, UB in runtime.
```

To statically check bounds array should be passed as reference:

```c++
int a[3] = {1, 2, 3};

void accept(int (&v)[4]) {
   v[3] = 1;
}

accept(a);   // Doesn't compile.
```

This sample still has problems, as arrays of greater than reference array length can't be passed in:

```c++
int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

void accept(int (&v)[4]) {
   v[3] = 1;
}

accept(a);   // Doesn't compile, semantically valid.
```

To allow passing of arrays with length >= acceptable one and do compile time bounds checks SFINAE can be used:

```c++
int three[]{1, 2, 3};
int four[]{1, 2, 3, 4};
int five[]{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

template<size_t length>
std::enable_if_t<(length >= 4)> accept(int (&v)[length]) {
   v[3] = 1;
}
```

```c++
accept(three);   // Doesn't compile
accept(four);     // Ok
accept(five);      // Ok
```

Apply ^ in this commit to check frustum / corners array bounds in compile time.
